        "title": {
          "en": "One, two or more ideas"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Arun said, \"I play. I play and she reads. I play when she reads.\"\n\"Each sentence is built differently,\" said the teacher."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "clause",
          "compound",
          "complex"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A simple sentence has one main clause: I play cricket. A compound sentence joins two main clauses with a conjunction like and, but, or: I play and she reads.\nA complex sentence has a main clause and a subordinate clause that cannot stand alone: I play when she reads. When she reads is the subordinate clause.\nCommon joining words for subordinate clauses are because, when, if, although and while."
        }
      },
